# Illustrations

Use an illustration to show a simple picture of something complex. A drawing can leave out everything except the part people need, which a photograph cannot do.

## When to use an illustration

Use an illustration:

- to show a physical action, such as pressing and holding the pairing button on a door sensor until its light flashes
- to help people find something on a device, such as where the model number is on the label under a smart plug

These help when a household adds a new device or reports a fault. The steps still go in text, next to the illustration.

If people need to see the real thing in their own home, use a [photograph](images-photography.html) instead. [Illustration](illustration.html) explains how an illustration differs from an icon or a photograph.

## Creating illustrations

Use as few shapes and colours as you can to get the message across. For the pairing button, you might draw only the sensor, the button, a fingertip and the light. Leave out the door, the room and the hub.

[Creating illustrations](illustration-creating.html) takes you through each step, from deciding the message to reviewing the drawing.

Save the illustration as an SVG and show it on a Picture card next to the card it explains, such as under the instructions for adding a door sensor. [File formats and exporting](illustration-formats.html) explains where to put the file. [Illustration accessibility](illustration-accessibility.html) explains when the illustration needs alternative text and how to keep its labels in text.

### Colour

Take colours from the illustration palette. [Illustration colours](colour-illustrations.html) owns the palette, the base colour and how many colours to use.

If you need more colours, use a lighter or darker shade of a palette colour, not a new colour. [Colour pairing](colour-illustrations.html#colour-pairing) explains how to use shades to separate the front of a drawing from the back.

### Flat colour

Use flat blocks of colour, with no shadows or gradients. [Illustration principles](illustration-principles.html) explains the flat style and how to show depth by overlapping shapes.

To show a feature that is set into a device, such as the reset hole on a hub, overlap shapes or use a darker shade of the case colour. Do not add a shadow.

### A set of illustrations

If you draw several illustrations for one dashboard or guide, use the same colours, line widths and corner shapes in all of them. A set of 3 pairing steps should look like 3 pictures of the same device, not 3 different devices.

### People

Leave people out unless the task needs them. Most tasks need no more than a hand, such as a fingertip on the pairing button. [Hands and gestures](illustration-hands.html) explains how to draw one.

If you need to show people, think about everyone who lives in or visits a home. Do not show only one kind of person. Showing only one age, gender or ethnicity can leave people out and repeat harmful stereotypes. [Characters](illustration-characters.html) explains how to show a range of people.

